  A little more seriously, your class, race, and starting stats
will make a LOT of difference in melee combat.  As will luck, 
alignments, etc.  


  I found out as a chaotic in Minetown that a cockatrice corpse 
took out the first 2/3 peacefuls no problem.  After that it 
missed over and over until a shopkeeper killed me.  (But surely 
I'd have gotten him on the next try. ;^)




  First off, the bad thing about melee combat is that if the
enemy is in range, *so are you*.




  Valks, barbs, and cavemen are best suited for the "run up and 
hit something" style of play.  Even they can be hamstrung by
low stats.


  Rogues should throw daggers early and often.  Just ask SMG:

<http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/913e48e42fb753b2?dmode=source&hl=en>



  Wizards should hide behind pets and use force bolt on 
approaching tough monsters.  Pick up and #enhance daggers while 
doing this until the Rogue approach of "throw daggers early and 
often" can be implemented.  Then they should hide behind pets
and throw daggers early and often.  


  Rangers should melee popcorn monsters and leave large monsters
quivering with arrows.  (Non-Elvish rangers should move every 
time a monster hits them to "re-activate" the cloak of 
displacement.)


  Tourists should emulate early Ranger tactics by meleeing 
popcorn monsters and darting tougher monsters.  (+2 darts are
formidable if thrown early enough.)



  NB: Rogues, Rangers, Tourists and Wizards therefore need to 
see/detect hostile monsters at a distance.  This is why some 
players value magic lamps more as a permanent light source
than as a potential wish.



  Healers are great for keeping pets (and themselves) alive.


  All classes can use pets to great advantage.  Don't depend on
pets to get you out of trouble.  Use them to keep out of trouble
instead.  (Think of a pet as a ranged weapon that's self mobile 
and targets for itself.)


  All classes can pick up and throw rocks.  Rocking floating 
eyes, yellow mold, blue jellies, brown/red molds and other
monsters with defensive attacks is recommended.


  All classes can try on every piece of non-cursed armor they
find.  My starting wizards will wear a cloak of displacement
if found.  Even taking off the "Wizard Security Blanket" (CoMR)
to do so.  (Low AC will compensate for a number of sins.)






